\chapter{Introduction}

International students represent a large and important part of the United States higher education system. The Open Doors 2025 release reported that 1,177,766 international students studied at U.S. higher education institutions during the 2024/25 academic year, representing about 6 percent of the total U.S. higher education population \cite{iie2025opendoors}. NAFSA also reported that international students contributed \$42.9 billion and supported 355,736 jobs during the 2024/25 academic year \cite{nafsa2025economic}. These numbers show that international students are not a small edge case. They are a major student population with financial lives that deserve tools designed around their actual constraints.

The financial experience of an international student is different from the experience assumed by most personal finance applications. A domestic budgeting tool usually expects one primary currency, steady income, recurring monthly bills, and unrestricted employment. International students often work with two currencies, receive support from family or scholarships at irregular times, and plan around large academic expenses such as tuition, rent deposits, health insurance, and international travel. Employment rules add another layer of complexity. For example, U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services states that F 1 students may work on campus up to 20 hours per week while school is in session and may work full time during vacation periods when eligible \cite{uscis2025employment}. This means that income is not only a financial variable. It is also connected to academic calendars and visa compliance.

These conditions create a planning problem rather than only a tracking problem. A student does not simply need to know that food spending increased last month. They need to understand whether a future tuition payment will create a shortfall, whether a flight home is affordable, how much rent and food will leave after a scholarship is received, and whether planned work hours remain within a safe range. These questions are forward looking. They require transaction history, expected expenses, academic timing, income assumptions, and uncertainty to be considered together.

Existing tools usually split these needs across separate systems. A student may use a banking application for transactions, a spreadsheet for tuition planning, university pages for visa rules, tax websites for filing guidance, and group chats for informal advice. This fragmentation increases the amount of work required from the student and makes important details easier to miss. Research on international student adjustment has also identified financial stress as a meaningful part of the student experience, connected with broader academic and social adjustment challenges \cite{mallinckrodt1992social}. The motivation for Spendemic comes from this gap: international students need a financial system that understands both money and student context.

Spendemic is a full stack AI financial planning web application built for international students studying in the United States. The name combines spend and endemic, reflecting the idea that money concerns tend to follow students across many parts of their academic life. The system helps users record income and expenses, manage category budgets, track savings goals, configure forecast assumptions, monitor work hour awareness, import transactions, upload receipts, ask an AI assistant for guidance, and view forecasts of future spending. The detailed implementation of these modules is discussed in Chapter 3. At the introductory level, the key idea is that Spendemic treats the academic calendar, visa constraints, currencies, scholarships, rent, food, travel, and graduation timeline as first class inputs instead of treating them as unrelated notes.

The project also explores an important engineering question: how can an AI based financial application remain useful when user data is limited and production infrastructure is constrained? New users may have little or no transaction history, which makes ordinary forecasting difficult. At the same time, powerful forecasting models may be too heavy for free or low cost hosting environments. Spendemic addresses this by combining Amazon Chronos 2, Meta Prophet, and a statistical fallback model. The system can use Chronos 2 when local model serving is available through ngrok, use Prophet on the hosted backend when Chronos 2 is unavailable, and still produce a basic forecast through exponential smoothing when neither advanced model is available. This approach reflects one of the main lessons of the project: responsible AI features should degrade gracefully rather than fail silently.

\section{Problem Context}

The core problem is not only that international students spend money differently. The deeper issue is that their financial decisions are shaped by overlapping systems. Tuition follows the semester calendar. Rent and utilities follow monthly billing cycles. Family support may follow a home country banking schedule. Exchange rates change the real value of transferred money. On campus work is limited during academic terms and may expand during breaks. Health insurance and travel may appear as large one time expenses. A useful planning tool for this audience must therefore combine predictable recurring costs, irregular academic expenses, income constraints, and currency conversion into one view.

This project treats those factors as structured application data. Instead of asking the forecasting model to infer everything from past transactions, Spendemic collects student specific context during onboarding, settings, and forecast setup. This context includes rent, food estimate, utilities, tuition, scholarship, travel, income, work hours, academic breaks, currencies, and graduation date. Later chapters explain how these values are stored and used in the forecasting pipeline. In the introduction, the important point is that the application is designed from the user problem backward rather than from a generic finance template forward.

\section{Motivation}

The motivation for Spendemic came from the observation that most financial tools are retrospective. They are good at showing where money went, but weaker at answering what a student can safely do next. For international students, the next decision often matters more than the last category chart. A student may need to decide whether to accept extra hours, whether to delay a purchase, whether to save toward a goal this month, or whether a future travel expense will conflict with tuition. These decisions require forecasts that are understandable rather than mysterious.

The project was also motivated by the need to build an end to end system rather than a standalone model. Forecasting is only valuable if the surrounding product collects the right data, validates it, stores it reliably, shows uncertainty honestly, and gives users a way to act on the result. For that reason, Spendemic includes authentication, transaction management, import handling, exchange rate caching, receipt uploads, budget alerts, settings, community support, AI assistance, deployment, and documented limitations. The model is important, but the system around the model is what makes it useful.

\section{Project Objectives}

The main objectives of this project are:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Student specific financial planning:} Build a financial management application tailored to international student constraints, including tuition cycles, scholarships, home and study currencies, rent, food, travel, academic breaks, and visa related work hour restrictions.

    \item \textbf{AI powered forecasting with limited history:} Integrate forecasting models that estimate future spending even when users have sparse transaction data. The current implementation uses Amazon Chronos 2 as the primary model, Meta Prophet as the secondary model, and a statistical fallback when advanced models are unavailable.

    \item \textbf{Multi currency and transaction intelligence:} Support transactions in multiple currencies, cache exchange rates, store normalized values for aggregation, and use AI assisted classification to process inconsistent CSV and Excel files.

    \item \textbf{Transparent predictions:} Display confidence bands, factor breakdowns, covariate sources, missing field warnings, and forecast explanations so users can understand what influenced the forecast.

    \item \textbf{Production oriented implementation:} Demonstrate a deployable full stack application using modern frontend, backend, database, authentication, API, and deployment practices while documenting limitations honestly.

    \item \textbf{Living documentation:} Maintain the report as an active document that records design decisions, bugs, trade offs, improvements, and next steps throughout the project lifecycle.
\end{enumerate}

