\chapter{Literature Review}

This chapter reviews the background that influenced the design of Spendemic. The project sits at the intersection of personal finance management, international student support, time series forecasting, AI assisted classification, and full stack web application deployment. The literature and existing tools show that many parts of the problem have been studied separately, but few systems combine them in a student centered financial planning platform.

\section{Personal Finance Management Tools}

Commercial personal finance tools have made expense tracking more accessible. Applications such as Mint, YNAB, PocketGuard, and similar products organize transactions into categories, show budget progress, and help users understand where their money went. These tools are useful, but they usually assume that the user has stable income, domestic banking access, a primary currency, and a predictable month to month financial pattern.

International students often break these assumptions. Tuition payments can be large and irregular. Rent deposits can distort the first month. Scholarship payments may be credited after a delay. Family support may arrive from a different country and must be converted into the study currency. Work hours may be limited by visa rules. Academic breaks may change income and spending. A normal category budget does not fully answer the question of whether a student can survive the next semester.

Spendemic borrows the useful parts of personal finance management, including transaction tracking, category budgets, savings goals, and monthly summaries. It then extends them by adding a structured student context. Fields such as visa type, maximum work hours, university, home currency, study currency, scholarship amount, graduation date, rent, food estimate, tuition due, travel home, and academic break flags are part of the actual data model. They are not treated as notes.

\section{International Student Financial Challenges}

International student financial stress is not only a personal budgeting issue. It is connected to academic performance, immigration compliance, and overall adjustment. Mallinckrodt and Leong identified financial problems as one of the important stressors in international student adjustment research \cite{mallinckrodt1992social}. In practical terms, financial stress can reduce academic focus, increase dependency on emergency support, and make students more vulnerable to poor employment or lending decisions.

The financial experience of international students is also shaped by rules. A student on an F 1 visa may need to understand on campus employment limits, CPT and OPT rules, and the consequences of unauthorized work. The student may also need to file taxes as a nonresident alien, understand a university health insurance requirement, open a bank account without a Social Security Number, and build credit history from scratch. These needs are often handled by different offices and websites. Spendemic does not replace official guidance, but it provides a single application space where student financial planning and practical resources can coexist.

\section{Time Series Forecasting for Student Finance}

Time series forecasting estimates future values based on past observations and, in some cases, external variables. In a personal finance context, the target variable may be weekly or monthly spending. The difficulty is that student spending is noisy and event driven. A single month may include a security deposit, a flight ticket, or tuition. Another month may be low because the student traveled home or stayed with family. Traditional moving averages are simple, but they cannot represent uncertainty well and may overreact to outliers.

A useful student finance forecast should satisfy several properties. It should work with limited data, because new users may not have a long transaction history. It should expose uncertainty, because a single point estimate can create false confidence. It should respond to known future events, such as tuition or travel. It should also be explainable enough for a user to understand why the predicted amount is high or low.

\section{Amazon Chronos 2 and Foundation Models}

Foundation models for time series are a recent development. Chronos, introduced by Ansari et al., converts real valued time series into token sequences using scaling and quantization, then trains language model architectures on those sequences \cite{ansari2024chronos}. The key benefit is zero shot forecasting. The model can produce forecasts on new time series without being trained specifically for that user or domain.

Chronos is relevant to Spendemic because of the cold start problem. A newly arrived international student may have only a few weeks of transaction data. A supervised model trained only on that user's history would not be reliable. A pretrained model can produce a reasonable first forecast earlier, and the forecast improves as more real user transactions are logged.

However, Chronos 2 also creates a design challenge. It is primarily a univariate model, meaning it forecasts from the past values of the target series. It does not naturally accept named covariates such as tuition due, rent, scholarship, summer break, or travel home. Spendemic solves this by converting future covariates into synthetic anchor values, then using those anchors to influence the sequence that reaches the model. This approach is not perfect, but it allows domain knowledge to affect a general purpose model without fine tuning.

\section{Prophet and Interpretable Forecasting}

Prophet, introduced by Taylor and Letham, is a forecasting procedure designed for business time series with trend, seasonality, and calendar effects \cite{taylor2018forecasting}. Prophet is useful because it is relatively easy to deploy and interpret. It decomposes a forecast into understandable components rather than acting as a fully opaque neural model.

In Spendemic, Prophet serves as the secondary model. It is a good match for the Render backend because it can run without a GPU and with less infrastructure than Chronos 2. Prophet is also useful for students who have only a few months of data, because it can model broad trend and seasonal structure without requiring a large neural training process. The current user interface labels this model as Prophet by Meta, while some internal API names still reflect an earlier plan to use an LSTM benchmark.

\section{LSTM Forecasting}

Long Short Term Memory networks were introduced by Hochreiter and Schmidhuber to model long range dependencies in sequences \cite{hochreiter1997lstm}. LSTMs have been widely used for financial and sequential forecasting. Fischer and Krauss demonstrated LSTM based forecasting in financial markets using daily equity returns \cite{fischer2018deep}. LSTMs can learn temporal patterns, but they usually need enough historical data and careful tuning.

For Spendemic, LSTM is important as background and as an originally planned benchmark. However, it is not the best first model for the current product because each student may have limited data. The deployed model comparison is better framed around Chronos 2, Prophet, and the statistical fallback. A formal LSTM benchmark remains possible later when enough user histories are available.

\section{AI Assistants and Classification}

Large language models have changed how applications can interpret natural language input. Spendemic uses Claude Haiku for two practical tasks: chat assistance and transaction import classification. In the chat assistant, the model receives financial profile context, navigation paths, and rules requiring confirmation before any action is executed. In the import pipeline, ambiguous merchant descriptions and category labels can be classified into the app's category system.

This is a different use of AI from forecasting. The forecasting model predicts numeric spending. The language model interprets user intent and text. Both are useful, but they require different safety designs. For chat actions, the assistant must not create transactions or budgets without user confirmation. For import classification, parse failures must fall back to a safe category such as OTHER rather than crashing the import process.

