\section{Database Design}

The PostgreSQL database is designed to store both transactions and the wider student context needed for forecasting. SQLAlchemy ORM models define tables, relationships, indexes, and constraints. Alembic migrations version schema changes over time.

\paragraph*{User Model.}

The User model stores identity, profile, address, financial profile, academic context, loan information, preferences, and security fields. Important fields include home currency, study country currency, monthly income, scholarship amount, scholarship frequency, university, student status, visa type, maximum work hours per week, graduation date, summer break dates, winter break dates, onboarding completion, and email verification.

This model matters because Spendemic forecasts are not based only on transactions. A student's expected graduation date can change the forecast horizon. Currency fields affect conversion logic. Visa type and work hour limits affect the Visa and Work tab. Scholarship and income fields can fill missing forecast context values.

\paragraph*{Session Model.}

The UserSession table stores every JWT issued to a user. Each token contains a unique \texttt{jti} claim. On every authenticated request, the backend checks whether the session exists and has not been revoked. Logout marks the session as revoked rather than waiting for token expiration. This design improves security because a token can be invalidated immediately.

\paragraph*{Transaction Model.}

The Transaction model records income and expense events. Each row stores amount, currency, normalized USD amount, transaction type, category, description, transaction date, recurring flags, recurring parent ID, generated child status, receipt URL, notes, and timestamps. The normalized USD column is important because aggregation and forecasting should not repeatedly perform currency conversion at query time.

\paragraph*{ForecastContext Model.}

ForecastContext is the most important domain specific table in the project. Each row represents one user and one future month. It stores covariates such as hours per week, hourly rate, break hourly rate, break hours per week, summer break, winter break, travel home, travel cost, tuition due, scholarship received, exchange rate, health insurance, rent, income amount, food estimate, and utilities estimate.

The uniqueness constraint on \texttt{user\_id}, \texttt{year}, and \texttt{month} ensures that each user has at most one forecast setup row for a month. This makes the forecast pipeline easier to reason about because there is a single source of future context for each period.

