\section{FAQ Moderation Methodology}

FAQ submissions pass through three moderation layers. The first layer is a hardcoded blocklist with simple normalization for common evasions. The second layer uses the better profanity library. The third layer optionally calls the OpenAI Moderation API. These layers catch different types of content. A simple blocklist is fast and predictable. A profanity library catches broader vocabulary. A moderation model can identify more nuanced harmful content.

\section{Datasets and Tools}

Spendemic does not use a public static dataset as the primary application data source. The main data is generated by users through transaction entry, transaction import, forecast setup, budgets, goals, and settings. For testing, sample student transaction data can be created manually or imported from CSV and Excel files.

The development tools include VS Code, GitHub, Vercel, Render, Neon, ngrok, Postman or browser based API testing, and local virtual environments. The backend uses Alembic migrations for schema changes. The frontend uses Vite for development and production builds. The updated repository README documents this split deployment clearly: Vercel hosts the React single page application, Render hosts the FastAPI backend, Neon provides PostgreSQL, and ngrok exposes the local Chronos 2 model server when the full forecasting stack is running. The README also separates full local dependencies from production dependencies through \texttt{requirements.txt} and \texttt{requirements-prod.txt}, so Render does not need to install the heavy Torch and Chronos packages for the normal hosted backend.



