\section{Non Functional Requirements}

The non functional requirements are equally important because the application handles financial data and is intended for real users. The system must be reliable, understandable, secure, and deployable within the constraints of a student project.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Usability:} The interface should be understandable for a student who may not be familiar with budgeting terminology. This influenced the use of summary cards, emojis for categories, progress bars, confirmation cards, and explanatory forecast panels.
    \item \textbf{Security:} Authentication tokens should be revocable. Passwords should be hashed. Protected routes should require a valid authenticated user. Uploaded files should be associated with the correct user.
    \item \textbf{Transparency:} Forecasts should not be shown as unexplained numbers. The system should show inputs, warnings, uncertainty, and factor breakdowns.
    \item \textbf{Performance:} Normal UI pages should load quickly even if the ML model is not available. Heavy model loading should not block the backend health endpoint.
    \item \textbf{Maintainability:} Backend routers, database models, frontend pages, and reusable components should be separated so that future features can be added without rewriting the application.
    \item \textbf{Graceful degradation:} If Chronos 2 is unavailable, the application should still provide Prophet or statistical forecasts instead of failing completely.
\end{itemize}

\section{End to End Data Flow}

The typical data flow begins with onboarding. The user enters profile, academic, currency, income, rent, and food information. The backend stores this information in the User table and creates initial ForecastContext rows. The user then records transactions manually or through import. Each transaction is stored with original currency and normalized USD amount. Budgets and goals are created against this transaction history.

When the user opens the Reports page, the frontend requests a forecast from the backend. The backend aggregates historical expenses, loads future ForecastContext rows, fills missing values from profile and job data, fetches or reuses exchange rates, applies break and graduation rules, selects the appropriate model, and returns predictions with metadata. The frontend renders charts, cards, warnings, and explanations.

