\section{Receipt Upload and File Handling}

Receipt upload is attached to the transaction workflow. The user can upload a receipt when creating or editing a transaction. The backend uploads files directly to Cloudflare R2 object storage using the S3-compatible API via boto3. The database stores the resulting public CDN URL. Files are served from R2 and persist across server restarts and redeployments.

\section{Data Validation}

Validation exists at multiple layers. The frontend validates required form fields, password strength, date inputs, amount inputs, and duplicate transactions. The backend validates request bodies through Pydantic schemas and database constraints. For example, the ForecastContext table enforces uniqueness for user, year, and month. Transaction categories and currencies use enumerations. Authentication protected endpoints require a valid current user.

This layered validation matters because frontend checks alone are not sufficient. A user can bypass frontend validation by sending a direct API request. Backend validation protects the database and application logic.

\section{Security Considerations}

Spendemic is not a banking application, but it still handles sensitive financial behavior. The current security design includes password hashing, JWT sessions, session revocation, protected routes, CORS configuration, and user based filtering on all private data. A user should only be able to access their own transactions, budgets, goals, jobs, forecast context, and FAQ submissions.

Future security work should include stronger rate limiting, persistent OTP storage, audit logs for sensitive changes, virus scanning for uploads, signed URLs for files, and more formal secrets management.

\section{User Interface Design Principles}

The user interface uses a dark teal, warm cream, and gold palette. This was chosen to feel related to money and trust without feeling like a generic banking dashboard. The visual style uses rounded cards, progress bars, small animations, and clear sectioning. The app intentionally avoids excessive animation libraries. Many motion details are implemented with CSS keyframes and small JavaScript interactions.

A favorite design detail is the decorative SVG money plant along the sidebar. It serves no functional purpose, but it makes the app feel more personal. This matters because financial apps can feel stressful. A softer visual identity can make the experience less intimidating for students.

