\section{Evaluation Metrics}

Evaluating a financial forecasting system requires thinking carefully about what ``good'' means for the specific audience. For a general-purpose time-series benchmark, the goal is raw predictive accuracy. For Spendemic, accuracy alone is not enough. A forecast that is numerically close but unexplained is not useful to a student making a real budget decision. A forecast that is technically correct but produces unactionably wide uncertainty bands is not useful either. The evaluation therefore spans three categories: quantitative point accuracy, confidence band calibration, and qualitative usability.

\subsection{Point Accuracy Metrics}

Point accuracy metrics compare the forecast's predicted value $\hat{y}_i$ for each period against the actual observed spending $y_i$.

\paragraph*{Mean Absolute Error (MAE).}

\begin{equation}
\text{MAE} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left|y_i - \hat{y}_i\right|
\end{equation}

MAE is the average absolute difference between what the model predicted and what actually happened, measured in dollars. If MAE is \$47 on monthly forecasts, the model is off by roughly \$47 on a typical month. This is directly interpretable without any transformation, which matters when explaining the forecast's reliability to a non-technical user. MAE treats all errors equally: a \$10 miss and a \$100 miss contribute to the average in proportion to their actual size.

\paragraph*{Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).}

\begin{equation}
\text{RMSE} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(y_i - \hat{y}_i\right)^2}
\end{equation}

RMSE squares each error before averaging, which means large errors are penalized much more heavily than small ones. If a model is mostly close but occasionally very wrong, RMSE will be noticeably higher than MAE. For a financial planning application, this distinction matters: a large underprediction can cause a student to believe they have budget headroom they do not actually have, leading to a spending decision that puts them in deficit. RMSE $>$ MAE by a large margin is a warning sign that the model has occasional large failures even if most months are predicted well.

\paragraph*{Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE).}

\begin{equation}
\text{MAPE} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\left|y_i - \hat{y}_i\right|}{y_i} \times 100
\end{equation}

MAPE expresses error as a percentage of the actual value. This is useful for comparing accuracy across users who have very different spending levels: a \$50 error means something different for a student spending \$600 a month versus one spending \$3{,}000 a month. MAPE normalizes for scale, making cross-user comparison fair. A MAPE below 15\% is considered a reasonable threshold for this application, meaning the model is within one month's worth of \$150 error on a \$1{,}000 typical month.

\subsection{Confidence Band Calibration}

Spendemic's Chronos 2 model outputs probabilistic forecasts as quantile estimates rather than a single predicted value. The reported confidence band uses quantile levels 0.2 and 0.8, forming a 60\% prediction interval. Calibration measures whether the stated confidence matches observed reality.

\paragraph*{Coverage Probability.}

For a 60\% prediction interval to be calibrated, the actual observed value should fall inside the interval approximately 60\% of the time across all forecast periods. Formally:

\begin{equation}
\text{Coverage} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\left[Q_{0.2,i} \leq y_i \leq Q_{0.8,i}\right]
\end{equation}

where $Q_{0.2,i}$ and $Q_{0.8,i}$ are the lower and upper band values for period $i$, and $\mathbf{1}[\cdot]$ is the indicator function that equals 1 when the condition is true. A coverage of 0.85 on a stated 60\% interval means the band is too wide, which is a form of overconfidence in the uncertainty. A coverage of 0.40 means the band is too narrow and is understating how uncertain the forecast is.

A 60\% interval was chosen deliberately over the more common 80\% or 90\% because for student budgeting, an excessively wide band is not actionable. If the forecast says spending will be between \$400 and \$3{,}200, no useful budget decision can be made from that range. The 60\% interval, presented honestly alongside the coverage explanation in the interface, gives a tighter and more useful planning range.

\subsection{Evaluation Challenges Specific to This Project}

Standard machine learning evaluation uses a held-out test set with many examples. This project faces a structural constraint: each user's transaction history is short. A student who joined three months ago has at most three monthly data points. Evaluating a model on three observations is not statistically meaningful in the same way as a standard benchmark.

The evaluation strategy therefore uses what is available. For users with six or more months of history, the final month is held out as a test period and the model is evaluated on that single month's prediction. For users with fewer than six months, error estimates from the model's own uncertainty output are used as a proxy. Aggregated across many users over time, the per-user single-month holdouts form a meaningful distribution of errors. The table in Section~\ref{tab:model_comparison_placeholder} reserves space for these aggregated results once sufficient transaction histories have accumulated.

A second challenge is that the synthetic covariate anchors introduced into the training sequence are known to the model but not real transactions. Error metrics computed on months that included synthetic anchors should be interpreted differently from months where the model was working from purely observed history.

\subsection{Qualitative Evaluation Dimensions}

Beyond numeric error metrics, the system is evaluated on the following qualitative dimensions.

\textbf{Transparency.} Does the user understand what drove the forecast? The factor breakdown card, data sources panel, and missing fields warning exist specifically to make the model interpretable. A technically accurate forecast that the user cannot explain is less useful than a slightly less accurate forecast they trust.

\textbf{Fallback reliability.} When Chronos 2 is unavailable, does Prophet produce a reasonable result? When Prophet is also unavailable, does the statistical fallback at minimum produce a plausible estimate? The fallback chain is evaluated by deliberately disabling each model level and confirming the system degrades gracefully rather than failing.

\textbf{Actionability of the confidence band.} A band width of more than 2.5 times the median forecast value is considered unactionable for planning purposes. Post-processing caps in the implementation enforce this, but the evaluation checks whether real outputs are staying within the intended range.

\textbf{Cold start behavior.} A new user with no transaction history receives a forecast seeded from their onboarding inputs. The evaluation checks whether this synthetic seed produces a plausible starting estimate rather than an extreme value, and whether the forecast improves measurably after two, four, and six real months of data are available.

\chapter{Discussion}

This chapter discusses the current implementation, design decisions, trade offs, bugs fixed, limitations, and planned evaluation. The discussion is written as a living project record, because many of the most important lessons came from integration work rather than from a single isolated algorithm.

\section{Current Progress and Status}

Spendemic is implemented as an end to end full stack application. The deployed frontend is available on Vercel. The backend runs on Render. The database uses PostgreSQL. The application supports authentication, onboarding, dashboard views, transactions, budgets, goals, reports, settings, FAQ, imports, alerts, and chat assistant functionality.

The current architecture differs from the earliest report draft. The original document described a simpler plan centered on Chronos and LSTM. The updated implementation uses Chronos 2, Prophet, and a statistical fallback. The original cloud plan also mentioned AWS services, but the active deployed application uses Vercel, Render, Neon, and ngrok.

\section{Design Decisions and Trade Offs}

\paragraph*{FastAPI and PostgreSQL.}

FastAPI was selected because it supports automatic request validation, OpenAPI documentation, dependency injection, and clean integration with Pydantic schemas. PostgreSQL was selected because the data is relational. Users have transactions, budgets, goals, jobs, sessions, forecast contexts, and FAQ submissions. Referential integrity matters more than schema flexibility in this application.

\paragraph*{JWT Session Revocation.}

A simple JWT implementation can issue tokens that remain valid until expiry. Spendemic improves this by storing every issued token's \texttt{jti} in the database. This allows logout to revoke the session immediately. The trade off is that every authenticated request needs a database lookup, but the security benefit is worth it for a financial planning application.

\paragraph*{Chronos 2 and Fallback Models.}

Chronos 2 was selected because it can forecast with limited history and provides probabilistic output. The trade off is deployment complexity. The model is heavier than a normal backend dependency and is not suitable for a free tier Render environment. Prophet and the statistical fallback reduce this risk. The system can still produce a forecast when the best model is unavailable.

\paragraph*{Synthetic Covariate Anchors.}

The synthetic anchor strategy is one of the central design decisions. Chronos 2 does not directly accept named covariates. Instead of fine tuning the model, Spendemic converts future student context into spending anchors. This allows tuition, rent, scholarship, travel, and break information to influence the sequence. The limitation is that the model does not know which points are real and which are synthetic. This is acceptable for the current stage, but a true covariate aware model would be a future improvement.

\paragraph*{Client Side Currency Conversion.}

The backend stores normalized USD values for aggregation, while the frontend can display converted values using exchange rates. This makes summaries efficient and keeps user display flexible. The trade off is that the system must be careful about which currency is used for model input, user display, and imported transaction amounts.

\section{Bugs and Fixes}

Several important bugs were found and fixed during development.

\paragraph*{React Hook Order Violation.}

In the onboarding page, an early redirect for completed users was placed before hook declarations. This violated React's rule that hooks must be called in the same order on every render. Moving the redirect below all \texttt{useState} and \texttt{useEffect} calls fixed the issue.

\paragraph*{Render Cold Start During Login.}

Render's free tier can spin down after inactivity. A user trying to sign in immediately after a long idle period could experience a failed Google login because the backend was still waking up. Adding a longer timeout and one automatic retry made the login flow more reliable.

\paragraph*{Weekly Budget Window.}

The first weekly budget calculation used the budget start date as the weekly anchor. This meant a budget created on Tuesday reset on Tuesday. The correct behavior is a rolling seven day window. The query was changed to calculate spending from the current date minus seven days.

\paragraph*{Confidence Bands Too Wide.}

With sparse history, early forecasts produced confidence bands that were too wide to be actionable. Outlier clipping and band capping were added to keep uncertainty visible but useful.

\paragraph*{Assistant Confirmation.}

Early chat prompt versions could make the assistant sound as if an action had already been performed. The system prompt was changed so the assistant must frame actions as suggestions and wait for user confirmation. The frontend also treats action blocks as pending actions rather than completed work.

\section{Model Comparison}

\subsection{The Testing Problem}

The standard way to test a forecasting model is to show it past data and ask it to predict a future value you already know. You then measure how far off the prediction was. The challenge for this project is that real user transaction histories are short: a student who joined three months ago has three data points, which is not enough to run a meaningful accuracy test.

To work around this, the evaluation uses ten synthetic student spending profiles. Each profile is a realistic 18-month spending sequence, generated with a fixed monthly baseline, tuition spikes added to August and September, lower spending in June and July to reflect summer break, and random variation at a realistic level. These profiles simulate what a real user's history might look like after a year and a half of using the app.

\subsection{How the Test Works}

For each profile, the evaluation runs what is called a rolling-window test. It works like this:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Give the model the first 6 months of spending as history. Ask it to predict month 7. Compare the prediction to the actual value. Record the error.
    \item Now give it 7 months. Ask it to predict month 8. Record the error.
    \item Repeat until month 17.
\end{enumerate}

Across all ten profiles, this produces 110 individual prediction attempts. Each attempt has a known correct answer, so the error can be measured precisely. The three error metrics used are:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{MAE (Mean Absolute Error)} --- the average dollar amount the model was off by. If MAE is \$120, the model's predictions are typically \$120 away from reality. This is the most directly interpretable number.
    \item \textbf{RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error)} --- similar to MAE, but large errors count more heavily. An RMSE much higher than MAE means the model is mostly close but occasionally very wrong.
    \item \textbf{Coverage} --- the fraction of actual values that fell inside the model's stated confidence band. Since the app shows a 60\% confidence band, a well-calibrated model should have coverage near 0.60. Too low means the band is falsely narrow; too high means it is wider than it needs to be.
\end{itemize}

