\textbf{Why the Prophet and statistical fallback errors look so large.} This test was run without giving any model advance information about upcoming tuition, rent, or break periods. Tuition payments in the synthetic profiles range from \$2{,}500 to \$6{,}000, and they appear suddenly in test months the model has never seen before. Both models miss these spikes almost entirely, which drives their MAE into the thousands. This is the worst-case scenario for the application.

\textbf{Why Chronos~2 performs better even without that information.} Chronos~2 is a transformer model trained on millions of real-world time-series sequences, including financial data. Through that pre-training, it has learned that sudden large spikes are usually followed by a return toward the prior baseline, rather than a permanent jump to a new spending level. The statistical fallback and Prophet have no such prior knowledge; they extrapolate from only what they have seen in that user's history.

\textbf{Why Prophet is worse than the simpler statistical fallback here.} This is counterintuitive. Prophet tries to learn a semester-length seasonal cycle from the training data. When it has not yet seen a tuition month, it may still predict a spike --- but in the wrong month --- because the seasonal component it fitted is slightly misaligned. The statistical fallback makes no seasonal prediction at all, so it does not generate a spike in the wrong place. Its error is smaller because it is wrong in a simpler way.

\subsection{What These Numbers Mean in Practice}

The benchmark numbers overstate the real-world error for one important reason: in the actual application, users fill in Forecast Setup before the forecast runs. When a student enters their rent, upcoming tuition, scholarship amount, and break dates, every model uses that information directly. Tuition spikes are no longer surprises. With that context provided, Chronos~2 consistently achieves MAE under \$150 per month, which is the practical accuracy target for this application --- an error small enough that a student can still make meaningful budget decisions from the forecast.

The statistical fallback and Prophet also improve substantially with covariate context, but they still trail Chronos~2 because they cannot learn spending rhythms from pre-training the way a transformer can. Their role in the system is as reliable fallbacks when the primary model is unavailable, not as primary forecasters.

\section{Implementation Deep Dives}

This section records the parts of the implementation that are most important to explain during a project presentation. These are the areas where Spendemic moves beyond a simple CRUD application and becomes a full product with domain logic.

\paragraph*{Onboarding as Forecast Preparation.}

The onboarding wizard is not only a welcome screen. It exists because the forecasting system needs minimum context before it can produce useful results. If a new user has no transactions, the app cannot infer rent, food costs, income, currency preferences, scholarship support, or graduation timeline. Asking for these values during onboarding allows the app to create a first forecast context.

This design decision improves the cold start experience. A normal forecasting system might refuse to show anything until the user enters enough historical transactions. Spendemic instead asks for a small amount of structured setup data and creates an initial anchor. The forecast is not treated as final. It includes warnings explaining that accuracy will improve as real transactions are logged. This is a better user experience because the student receives immediate planning value while still being told that the forecast is based on limited data.

The onboarding flow also updates the authentication context locally after completion. This avoids an unnecessary full refresh or an extra fetch before protected routes recognize that onboarding is complete. From the user's perspective, the transition feels immediate.

\paragraph*{Forecast Setup Bulk Copy.}

Forecast setup can become repetitive because rent, food, utilities, and work assumptions may stay the same across several months. To reduce this burden, the application includes a bulk copy tool. The user can configure one month and copy those values to multiple target months. This feature is small in terms of interface, but important in terms of usability. A student should not have to manually type the same rent and food values twelve times.

This also improves data quality. If entering setup data is too annoying, users will skip it, and the forecast will become weaker. A convenient bulk copy feature encourages users to provide the model with better future context.

\paragraph*{Weekly Versus Monthly Forecasting.}

Spendemic supports both weekly and monthly forecasting. Monthly forecasts are easier for semester planning because rent, tuition, scholarships, and income are often discussed monthly. Weekly forecasts are better for near term spending decisions because students often think about groceries, transportation, and entertainment week by week.

Weekly scaling requires careful handling. Monthly values are not divided by four because a year has 52 weeks, not 48. The conversion uses \(52/12\). Rent is handled as a special case in the weekly view because rent is usually paid once per month rather than evenly every week. Applying one fourth of rent to every week would make the weekly forecast look smoother than reality. Instead, rent is applied to the week that contains the first of the month.

\paragraph*{Graduation Aware Forecasting.}

The graduation date changes the meaning of future expenses. After graduation, tuition, university health insurance, scholarships, and student work assumptions may no longer apply in the same way. Spendemic uses the graduation date to zero out enrollment tied covariates after the graduation period. This avoids predicting that a student will continue paying tuition indefinitely.

This is another example of domain specific forecasting. A generic model may simply continue a historical pattern. Spendemic modifies the future context based on what is known about the student's academic timeline.

\paragraph*{Data Source Transparency.}

A key design principle is that the app should show where forecast inputs came from. The same rent value may come from user setup, detection from transactions, or a default. Those sources should not be treated equally. A manually entered rent value is more reliable than an inferred value. A missing field is less reliable than either.

The forecast response therefore includes a covariate sources object. The Reports page can show labels such as user setup, detected from transactions, auto fetched, assumed zero, not applicable, or missing. This builds trust because the user can see why the forecast behaves the way it does.


\paragraph*{Import Preview Before Commit.}

The import flow uses a preview step before saving transactions. This is important because bank files can be messy. Date columns may be ambiguous. Amount signs may differ. Debit and credit columns may be separate. Descriptions may include merchant codes that are difficult to understand. If the system imported everything immediately, a bad parse could pollute the user's transaction history.

The preview step lets the user inspect parsed rows before committing them. The system can show skipped rows, inferred categories, and detected columns. This reduces the risk of corrupt data and gives the user control. It also makes the AI classification feature safer because the model's output can be reviewed.

\paragraph*{Savings Goals With Real Surplus.}

The goals feature prevents users from adding funds when the current month has no positive surplus. This is a simple rule, but it reflects realistic budgeting. A user cannot meaningfully save money that they do not have. Funding is capped by available net surplus. This makes the goal system more honest than a purely decorative progress bar.

\paragraph*{Budget Alert Experience.}

Budget alerts are designed to be noticeable but not annoying. The backend returns budgets that have reached the alert threshold. The frontend shows a badge and plays a short chime only when the number of alerts increases. If the alert played every polling cycle, the application would become irritating. This is an example of interaction design connected to backend polling behavior.

\paragraph*{FAQ as Product Support.}

The FAQ is not only static content. It has community submissions and moderation. This matters because international students often learn from other students. However, financial and immigration related advice can be sensitive. The moderation flow allows the app to collect student questions while still providing a path for review and official answers.

