\chapter{Conclusion and Future Work}

Spendemic is a purpose built AI financial planning application for international students. The project addresses a real gap in existing tools. International students need more than generic expense tracking. They need planning that understands currencies, tuition cycles, scholarships, rent, food, travel, academic breaks, visa work limits, and graduation timelines.

\section{Summary of Contributions}

The first contribution is a full stack application that integrates student financial tracking with domain specific context. Users can manage transactions, budgets, goals, imports, forecasts, settings, jobs, FAQ content, and assistant interactions in one system.

The second contribution is the ForecastContext model. This table translates future academic and financial assumptions into structured monthly covariates. It allows the forecasting pipeline to reason about upcoming tuition, rent, food, utilities, travel, health insurance, scholarships, break periods, income, and graduation.

The third contribution is the layered forecasting architecture. Chronos 2 provides the primary AI forecast. Prophet provides a hosted fallback. A custom statistical model ensures that the application can still produce output when AI models are unavailable. This design demonstrates practical resilience.

The fourth contribution is transparency. Spendemic does not simply display a prediction. It shows confidence bands, factor breakdowns, missing fields, data source labels, savings outlook, and warnings. This is important because financial predictions affect real student decisions.

The fifth contribution is honest engineering documentation. The project records bugs, trade offs, deployment constraints, and known limitations instead of hiding them. This makes the report stronger and more useful.

\section{Future Work}

\paragraph*{Redis for OTP and Session Support.}

The OTP store should be moved from process memory to Redis. Redis could also support rate limiting and short lived verification state.

\paragraph*{Backend Sync for Visa Work Hours.}

Visa work hour tracking should be persisted on the backend instead of relying on localStorage. This would allow users to access the same records across devices and reduce the risk of losing compliance history.

\paragraph*{Formal Model Evaluation.}

After collecting enough transaction history, Chronos 2, Prophet, the statistical fallback, and optional LSTM can be compared using MAE, RMSE, latency, and qualitative usefulness. The placeholder table in Chapter 4 should be filled with real results.

\paragraph*{Improved Covariate Aware Forecasting.}

A future version could use a model that directly accepts covariates rather than relying on synthetic anchors. This would make rent, tuition, scholarship, travel, and breaks explicit inputs instead of encoded spending anchors.

\paragraph*{More Advanced Assistant Workflows.}

The AI assistant can be extended to answer deeper financial questions, explain forecasts, update forecast setup after confirmation, and help users prepare semester plans. The key safety rule should remain: no financial action should be executed without user approval.

\paragraph*{Expanded Student Resource Hub.}

The FAQ and resource sections can be expanded with official links, university specific guidance, tax deadlines, banking checklists, and scholarship resources. The goal is not to replace official offices, but to make the student's path easier.

\section{Final Remarks}

Spendemic demonstrates that an AI financial planning application becomes more meaningful when it is designed for a specific user group. The project is not only about predicting expenses. It is about understanding the context behind those expenses and giving students a clearer view of the months ahead. By combining full stack development, time series forecasting, AI assistance, multi currency support, and transparent design, Spendemic provides a strong foundation for a practical international student financial guide.

